1
00:00:00,103 --> 00:00:05,711
Human Judgment in an Automated World A laboratory introduced a virtual assistant

2
00:00:05,724 --> 00:00:11,053
to summarize research papers It could scan a manuscript extract a theme

3
00:00:11,276 --> 00:00:16,421
and generate a brief exposition within seconds For a busy researcher the

4
00:00:16,434 --> 00:00:22,158
potential gain in productivity was substantial The tool could also classify material

5
00:00:22,171 --> 00:00:27,105
by category and reveal a pattern that a reader might overlook However

6
00:00:27,211 --> 00:00:31,947
an elegant summary could still contain a blunder The assistant might invent

7
00:00:31,961 --> 00:00:36,289
a reference confuse a theory with a proven theorem or confidently assert

8
00:00:36,303 --> 00:00:43,013
something unsupported A polished tone was no guarantee of sound reasoning Consequently

9
00:00:43,132 --> 00:00:47,421
the laboratory required a human review before any generated passage entered a

10
00:00:47,434 --> 00:00:53,132
formal report Researchers had to ascertain whether each citation existed and whether

11
00:00:53,145 --> 00:00:59,039
it supported the associated claim As useful as automation can be responsibility

12
00:00:59,053 --> 00:01:04,408
remains with the people who deploy it A sophisticated mechanism can facilitate

13
00:01:04,421 --> 00:01:10,342
analysis it cannot justify careless judgment The team's guiding principle was to

14
00:01:10,355 --> 00:01:16,079
preserve human oversight while using technology to lessen repetitive work They measured

15
00:01:16,092 --> 00:01:20,513
success by dependable results not by the mere rapidity of a response
